{"items":[{"gapId":"n0000062","nodes":[{"text":"Applying a lambda abstraction to an argument replaces x in t with u: (λx. t) u → t[x := u].","densityScore":0.94}]},{"gapId":"n0000098","nodes":[{"text":"One use can connect separately to an argument and a work component.","densityScore":0.71},{"text":"A second use can likewise connect separately to an argument and a work component.","densityScore":0.71}]},{"gapId":"n0000100","nodes":[{"text":"Two uses can share one intermediate share component.","densityScore":0.78},{"text":"The shared component can feed one argument.","densityScore":0.72}]},{"gapId":"n0000134","nodes":[{"text":"An interaction step either removes an active pair or lets it pass through while reconnecting nearby wires.","densityScore":0.87},{"text":"Distinct active pairs never share an agent because each agent has one principal port.","densityScore":0.9},{"text":"Perfect confluence gives a one-step diamond: two possible steps from N reach a common net after one more interaction each.","densityScore":0.94},{"text":"Distinct active-pair rewrites commute because they use distinct agents and leave the same nearby wiring in either order.","densityScore":0.91},{"text":"Confluence and termination are separate: some nets reduce forever.","densityScore":0.84},{"text":"When a core interaction order normalizes, every normalizing order reaches the same result in the same number of interactions.","densityScore":0.91},{"text":"Earlier optimal-reduction nets used indexed fans and delimiter agents to track sharing scopes.","densityScore":0.84},{"text":"Δ-Nets replaces separate sharing-scope bookkeeping with a variable-arity replicator carrying a level and per-port deltas.","densityScore":0.93},{"text":"A Δ-Net core contains fans, erasers, and replicators.","densityScore":0.86},{"text":"A fan has exactly two auxiliary ports and represents lambda-term application and abstraction structure.","densityScore":0.88},{"text":"An eraser has no auxiliary ports and marks an unused bound variable.","densityScore":0.84},{"text":"A replicator has any natural-number auxiliary arity and joins uses of one shared value.","densityScore":0.88},{"text":"A replicator level names its sharing context, while each auxiliary port delta adjusts a replica's level when it exits that port.","densityScore":0.92},{"text":"ΔL permits fans only; ΔA adds erasers; ΔI adds replicators; ΔK permits all three agents.","densityScore":0.9},{"text":"Linear terms have exactly one bound use, affine terms at most one, relevant terms at least one, and full terms any count.","densityScore":0.9},{"text":"Equal replicators formally require matching level, arity, and every port delta.","densityScore":0.9},{"text":"For well-formed nets translated from lambda terms, active replicators at the same level are already equal.","densityScore":0.87},{"text":"Annihilation removes equal agents and reconnects corresponding auxiliary ports.","densityScore":0.88},{"text":"Erasure deletes the distinct agent met by an eraser because the eraser has no auxiliary ports.","densityScore":0.85},{"text":"Distinct non-eraser agents commute, producing outgoing copies according to the other agent's auxiliary-port count.","densityScore":0.87},{"text":"Fan annihilation is beta-reduction in graph form.","densityScore":0.89},{"text":"When a replicator meets a fan, the replicator is copied through the fan's two auxiliary ports and the fan is copied through every replicator port.","densityScore":0.91},{"text":"Fan-replicator reconnection is determined by port identities: (Fᵢ)ⱼ ↔ (Rʲ)ᵢ.","densityScore":0.93}]},{"gapId":"n0000212","nodes":[{"text":"Equal replicators annihilate only when level, arity, and every port delta match; same level suffices for well-formed lambda translations.","densityScore":0.94},{"text":"Replicators at different levels commute rather than annihilate.","densityScore":0.88},{"text":"When lower-level R meets higher-level H, R creates one H replica per R auxiliary port.","densityScore":0.89},{"text":"Each H replica's new level equals H's old level plus the delta on the R port it exits.","densityScore":0.95},{"text":"H creates one exact copy of R per H auxiliary port; those R copies preserve R's level and deltas.","densityScore":0.92},{"text":"During different-level replicator commutation, auxiliary port j of each H replica connects to the matching port of Rʲ.","densityScore":0.87},{"text":"A port delta is an integer local level adjustment applied to every higher-level replica exiting through that port.","densityScore":0.9},{"text":"For R at level 2, an endpoint at level 2 gives delta 0 and an endpoint at level 5 gives delta +3.","densityScore":0.88},{"text":"An H replica at level 7 exits through delta 0 at level 7 and through delta +3 at level 10.","densityScore":0.9},{"text":"A delta may be negative, zero, or positive.","densityScore":0.75},{"text":"For each lambda fragment, the translation φ is a bijection between terms and canonical Δ-Nets.","densityScore":0.91},{"text":"The translation is inductive: translate smaller term fragments first and then wire them into larger fragments.","densityScore":0.85},{"text":"Every translated fragment has one incoming wire and zero or more outgoing wires; the outer net has one root.","densityScore":0.87},{"text":"A free variable becomes a named boundary node rather than a rewriting agent.","densityScore":0.84},{"text":"A bound variable occurrence is a wire whose endpoint later connects to its owning binder directly or through a sharing replicator.","densityScore":0.89},{"text":"An abstraction fan connects its first auxiliary port to the body and its second auxiliary port to the bound variable.","densityScore":0.88},{"text":"An unused bound variable connects the abstraction's variable port to an eraser in ΔA and ΔK.","densityScore":0.84},{"text":"A one-port replicator with zero delta is equivalent to a wire and is omitted from canonical construction.","densityScore":0.86}]},{"gapId":"n0000289","nodes":[{"text":"An abstraction's bound-variable port connects directly to one occurrence, to an eraser when unused, or to a replicator when several occurrences share one value.","densityScore":0.91},{"text":"An application fan connects its principal port to the function, first auxiliary port to the result, and second auxiliary port to the argument.","densityScore":0.91},{"text":"For an application [M N] at level ℓ, M has level ℓ and N has level ℓ + 1.","densityScore":0.93},{"text":"For an abstraction λx.M at level ℓ, its body M remains at level ℓ and a needed sharing replicator Rₓ has level ℓ + 1.","densityScore":0.94},{"text":"The whole translated term begins at level 0.","densityScore":0.79},{"text":"A bound occurrence's wire endpoint has the level reached by walking the source syntax to that occurrence.","densityScore":0.86},{"text":"Walking a lambda body or an application function preserves the current level; entering an application argument adds one.","densityScore":0.93},{"text":"A replicator port delta equals its connected occurrence level minus the replicator level.","densityScore":0.94},{"text":"For λx. x (g x), the two x occurrences have levels 0 and 2, while Rₓ has level 1 and deltas ⟨−1, +1⟩.","densityScore":0.92},{"text":"The linear term (λx.x)(λy.y) reduces by one fan annihilation to λy.y.","densityScore":0.86},{"text":"The term λx.x x uses a two-port replicator because one bound input has two consumers.","densityScore":0.88},{"text":"For [λx.x x]₀, the replicator has level 1, with function-use delta −1 and argument-use delta 0.","densityScore":0.91},{"text":"A level delta is neither a copy count nor a source-level variable value; it records relative sharing context at a consumer.","densityScore":0.91},{"text":"For λx. x (x (x x)), the binder replicator is at level 1 and its four occurrence deltas are ⟨−1, 0, +1, +2⟩.","densityScore":0.93},{"text":"The four occurrence endpoint levels in λx. x (x (x x)) are 0, 1, 2, and 3.","densityScore":0.88},{"text":"Applying λx. x (x (x x)) to N produces N (N (N N)) by beta-reduction.","densityScore":0.84}]},{"gapId":"n0000358","nodes":[{"text":"Translation produces canonical nets, while proper nets are intermediate nets reachable by zero or more Δ interactions.","densityScore":0.91},{"text":"Canonicalization restores a proper net to the canonical shape expected by back-translation.","densityScore":0.9},{"text":"In ΔA and ΔK, erasure canonicalization marks nodes reachable from the root and deletes unmarked material.","densityScore":0.91},{"text":"When deleting unreachable material leaves an exposed connection in the net, that connection is terminated by an eraser.","densityScore":0.85},{"text":"The erasure sweep may run at the end or earlier to save space; the paper recommends sweeping after applications of abstractions that ignore their arguments when memory matters most.","densityScore":0.89},{"text":"In ΔI and ΔK, consecutive replicators can merge when a local level test proves the later replicator is unpaired.","densityScore":0.88},{"text":"For consecutive replicators A and B joined through delta d, B is known unpaired when 0 ≤ lB − lA ≤ d.","densityScore":0.94},{"text":"Replicator merging preserves external endpoint levels while recomputing deltas relative to the surviving replicator's level.","densityScore":0.89},{"text":"In ΔK, unpaired replicator ports leading directly to erasers can be removed.","densityScore":0.86},{"text":"A remaining one-port unpaired replicator with delta zero collapses to a wire.","densityScore":0.88},{"text":"Canonicalization needs a schedule because it can touch ports that are not principal-to-principal active pairs.","densityScore":0.85},{"text":"The prescribed nonlinear-system schedule selects an outermost reducible pattern and then the leftmost candidate.","densityScore":0.89},{"text":"A reducible pair may run early when it will eventually reach leftmost-outermost position unchanged.","densityScore":0.82},{"text":"The full algorithm translates a term with φ, reduces through proper nets, normalizes and canonicalizes with Ω, then reads back with φ⁻¹.","densityScore":0.93},{"text":"Leftmost-outermost reduction avoids evaluating subnets that will later be erased.","densityScore":0.84},{"text":"Replicators represent shared use in the graph, avoiding repeated evaluation solely because an expression has multiple consumers.","densityScore":0.87},{"text":"Perfect confluence permits disjoint active pairs to choose and apply local interaction rules independently.","densityScore":0.89},{"text":"In ΔL, every proper net is canonical and n beta-reductions correspond to n interactions in any order.","densityScore":0.89},{"text":"ΔA requires erasure canonicalization; ΔI requires replicator merging; ΔK requires erasure, merge, and decay canonicalizations.","densityScore":0.9},{"text":"The three-agent interaction core has the one-step diamond property.","densityScore":0.88},{"text":"For nonlinear systems, the paper claims Church-Rosser confluence and optimality only after adding canonicalization and a global schedule.","densityScore":0.9},{"text":"Δ-Nets aims to represent each sharing context with a variable-arity replicator while using local interactions and limited global discipline to avoid preventable work.","densityScore":0.92}]}]}
